  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The probability of not making any Type-I errors (false positives) over n independent tests, each at level $ \\alpha $, is $ (1 - \\alpha)^n $. Hence, the probability, $ \\pi $, of having at least one false positive over the entire collection is simply one minus this or $$ \\pi=1-\\left(1-\\alpha\\right)^{n} $$",
      "source_page": 1233,
      "source_pages": [
        1233
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p004:b12",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p004:b13",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p004:b14"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          288.0,
          1231.0,
          584.0,
          1254.0
        ],
        [
          287.0,
          1261.0,
          1059.0,
          1333.0
        ],
        [
          598.0,
          1351.0,
          748.0,
          1377.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "cf12121d1ffeb6c40fe35233c49773f25cdfb75fe7a4c31c796e5ae3a919e158"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "If we solve for the $ \\alpha $ value required for each test, $$ \\alpha=1-(1-\\pi)^{1/n} $$",
      "source_page": 1233,
      "source_pages": [
        1233
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p004:b16",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p004:b17"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          288.0,
          1398.0,
          707.0,
          1422.0
        ],
        [
          590.0,
          1443.0,
          755.0,
          1469.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "76896834250b2e7b095694c4b9a465a5bef0f5df07c84d34d24cdca6ef60f86d"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "This is often called the Dunn-Šidák method. If we note that $ (1 - \\alpha)^n \\simeq 1 - n\\alpha $, we obtain the Bonferroni method by taking $$ \\alpha=\\pi/n $$",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b2",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p005:b3"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          205.0,
          902.0,
          254.0
        ],
        [
          477.0,
          255.0,
          558.0,
          277.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "4757398460e166b2345ad359ecb5981b117322862d9842a955664721f558b149"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Both Equations A4.3b and A4.4 are referred to as Bonferroni corrections. In the literature, $ \\pi $ is the family-wide error rate (FWER), while $ \\alpha $ is the comparison-wise error rate (CWER), also referred to as the point-wise significance level (PWSL).",
      "source_page": 1234,
      "source_pages": [
        1234
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
